I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction pattern first, then identify candidate phases and build a quantitative GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep the submitted CIFs, profile, statistics, report, and the exact final project mutually consistent.

The chemistry points toward manganese tungstate formation, but that alone is not enough: unreacted oxides or multiple Mn–W–O polymorphs could contribute. I’m now extracting the experimental peak positions and checking the supplied structure library against the full pattern, including minor peaks.

The observed peak set is not consistent with Mn₃WO₆ or either precursor oxide as the dominant phase. Its paired strongest reflections near 29.58/30.03° and the lines at 18.06, 35.67, and 53.15° are characteristic of wolframite-type MnWO₄; I’m checking this against every available Mn–W–O structure and testing whether the remaining lines require a second crystalline phase.

A near-pure MnWO₄ starting model is available from a hubnerite structure (3% Fe substitution); I’m converting that site to pure Mn and using the reported Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ doublet explicitly. The next refinement stages will separate lattice/zero-shift effects from profile broadening before I assess any unmatched intensity for minor phases.

The single wolframite phase accounts for essentially every observed line across 10–100°; I see no independent residual peak set that would justify a second phase. I’m now testing the low-angle axial-divergence and specimen-height terms explicitly, since those dominate the residual and otherwise bias the refined cell.

The displacement term is in micrometres in GSAS-II; correcting that unit exposed the expected physically meaningful starting range (hundreds of µm). The constrained profile comparison is still running, but phase identification is unchanged: the remaining discrepancies are line-shape/intensity effects, not unexplained Bragg peaks.

A closer residual check does reveal a weak, broad family near 22.7, 28.2, 32.8–33.7°, so I’m reopening the phase-count question. Those positions could represent residual WO₃ rather than line-shape error; I’ll test each plausible W-oxide and Mn-oxide model quantitatively and retain a second phase only if its full reflection set improves the fit coherently.

The second-phase comparison is taking longer because each candidate is being fully linked and refined against all 8,284 points, not just peak-matched. The decisive test is whether the weak scale remains positive and whether the candidate explains the 22.7/33.7/49.5/55.5° family together without creating absent peaks.

Monoclinic WO₃ is decisively supported: adding it lowers Rwp from about 10.8% to 4.54% and refines to ~15.3 wt%, while orthorhombic/tetragonal WO₃ models fit materially worse. I’m doing one final third-phase check against the precursor/likely by-product oxides; their scales will be constrained nonnegative so a tiny mathematical cancellation cannot masquerade as a real phase.

The third-phase check supports residual tetragonal Mn₃O₄ at about 4 wt%: it improves χ² by ~1,007 with only a small parameter increase and explains independent residuals near 31.8 and 59.3°, not just overlaps. The refined residual WO₃:Mn₃O₄ mass ratio also closely matches the 3:1 molar precursor stoichiometry after accounting for formed MnWO₄, which is a useful chemical cross-check.

The selected three-phase model is now stable. I refined the MnWO₄ cell and atomic coordinates, refined the cells of monoclinic WO₃ and hausmannite Mn₃O₄, and kept minor-phase internal coordinates fixed because their fractions do not support reliable unconstrained coordinate refinement. I’m packaging that exact model into the final GPX/CIF/profile/result/report set now.

